body:
Hm. It's not ideal that the engine has such a hard-coded reference to a feature added in the godot_openxr_vendors repo.

What do we gain by doing this check? I assume it wouldn't work anyway if the right stuff wasn't added to the `AndroidManifest.xml`, so why do we need to also check for it?

@m4gr3d @BastiaanOlij Do you guys remember why we have this?

@anton-moroz-snke:

> Just to clarify, I think that the permission string **PERMISSION_XR_EXT_eye_gaze_interaction** is working, but the feature for extension itself **XR_EXT_EYE_GAZE_INTERACTION_EXTENSION_NAME** isn't. It is supposedly something different from a permission check.

Ah, yeah, for `XR_EXT_EYE_GAZE_INTERACTION_EXTENSION_NAME`, the vendor repo is doing:

```
	if ((bool)ProjectSettings::get_singleton()->get_setting_with_override("xr/openxr/extensions/eye_gaze_interaction")) {
		features.append(EYE_GAZE_INTERACTION_FEATURE);
	}
```

So, it's effectively equivalent to the project setting. Since Godot is already checking the project setting, this seems unnecessary.

(And it's not working in your case, because that bit of code is duplicated for several different vendors, but not for Magic Leap.)

@BastiaanOlij:

> BUT skipping over the needed steps for the eye gaze interaction is something we need to do in core.

I still don't think we actually need to do this, though.

If we were to remove the check for the permission feature tag, then, yes, on platforms that require a permission, the extension wouldn't work if the permission wasn't granted via godot_openxr_vendors. But I think that's OK! It would mean our attempts to use the extension would fail, but we always need to be prepared for any OpenXR function call to fail.

If this was added because those failures led to error spam in the logs, I think it would be better to directly address that error spam, for example: we could print the offending failure only once (via `ERR_PRINT_ONCE()`) or give up on eye gaze interaction if we get enough failures.

Anyway, like @anton-moroz-snke mentions above, his issue isn't even about the feature tag added based on the permission, but the other one. So, perhaps we can at least agree about removing the check for that one :-)
